"A Token-Bucket & Leaky-Bucket Rate Limiter (Distributed)"

Phase1-- Concept to grasp: Fixed Windows, Sliding Windows,  Token Bucket,  Leaky Bucket
*In-memory first approach, to expose the algorithm behind them before Redis Implementation approach.

Phase2-- Clean Go architecture :  Http Handler ----  RateLimiter Interface ---- Bucket Implementation

Phase3-- Request, Extract ClientID, Ask Limiter,  Allowed,  Next Handler

Phase4-- Concurrency: Race conditions, mutexes, sync/atomic, false sharing, contention.  "Create bugs and fix bugs"

Phase5-- Redis: Http---Redis---Lua Script---Atomic Update

Phase6-- Distributed System: Instance A, B, C all sharing Redis

Phase7-- Compare Fixed window, Sliding window, Token Buckets, Leaky Bucket

Phase8-- Testing: Table driven test, Parallel tests, race detector, benchmarks

Phase9-- Go internals: Pointers, Recievers, Analysis, Heap vs Stack, Garbage collection, Memory Layouts, Cache friendliness, Alignmnent,
False sharing.

CODING STANDARD TO FOLLOW:
Keep Packages cohesive and responsiblities clear
Prefer composition over inheritance
Use interface where they provide value, not preemptively.
Accept context.Context as the first parameter for operations that may block or interact with external systems.
Return errors instead of panicking in library code.
Write table-driven tests.
Benchmark performance-critical paths.
Document exported types and functions.
Avoid global mutable state.
Measure before optimizing.

